# Custom Page Document Model Consolidation

## Context

The completed `typed_page_sections_redesign` built a second normalized custom-page
storage and API system, but deployed custom pages still read and write the proven
ordered JSON array in `pages.content`. The normalized feature flags are absent
from the local deployment and therefore default off. Despite that, every ordinary
page create/update transaction also projects the document into shadow tables.
The operator confirmed on 2026-08-16 that none of the backend migration/backfill/
cutover commands were run in production, so production never changed authority
away from the legacy custom-page document.

The 2026-08-16 planner/correctness inventory found:

- `home` has 7 active pages and every `pages.content` value is a JSON array.
- Its 23 sections contain 20 JSON-string configs and 3 object configs, plus 20
  numeric IDs and 3 string IDs.
- Live content includes the `features` alias and every interactive type (`form`,
  `qa`, `survey`, `poll`, and `vote`). These shapes do not all satisfy the
  normalized schema or the current strict save-time envelope.
- The default-off normalized subsystem nevertheless has 9 active shadow section
  rows, 4 shadow-run rows, 65 recorded compatibility writes, 4 auto-created page
  themes, no palette tokens, no normalized response rows, and one unresolved
  quarantine record. Only 3 active normalized section rows belong to pages whose
  shadow status is matched.
- The typed implementation contributes roughly 6,700 lines in its direct
  migration/storage/schema/generator/API artifacts before counting integration
  branches in the main page service, handler, builder, models, migrations, specs,
  tests, and the 545-line root `cmd/page-section-migrate` orchestrator.

This is not a request to restore the pre-redesign UI wholesale. The backend
section/component registries, JSON-backed interactive privacy and validation,
content-free realtime refresh, renderer registry, shared section frame, and
useful appearance controls must survive when they can operate directly on the
document model.

## Goal

- Make `pages.content` the sole custom-page definition and interactive-response
  authority again, with one browser save path and no background shadow writes.
- Remove the never-activated normalized cutover, backfill, typed mutation,
  generated contract, page-theme/palette, and orchestration machinery after a
  small defensive data check.
- Keep a small, explicit registry of supported section types, renderer ownership,
  defaults/capabilities that are actually used, integration validation, and
  interactive validation.
- Make the real document envelope honest about numeric-or-string legacy IDs,
  string-or-object configs, the `features` alias, interactive runtime fields, and
  unknown fields that must round-trip.
- Preserve existing pages byte-for-byte or semantically without requiring a
  content migration merely to keep editing them.

## Non-Goals

- Reverting commit `63668caa` wholesale; it also contains useful security,
  realtime, registry, renderer, and UI work.
- Replacing the page builder, `BlockRenderer`, or the JSON document with another
  persistence experiment.
- Removing the datasource/component registry or the legacy global
  `page_sections`/`page_items` configuration domain.
- Weakening interactive response authorization, privacy filtering, idempotency,
  record-preserving page saves, bounds, audit events, or clipboard/duplicate
  sanitization.
- Removing shared section presentation controls solely because they were
  introduced during the typed work.
- Retiring unrelated orphaned `page_interaction_*` tables without their own
  deployed-data proof.

## Keep / Simplify / Remove Boundary

| Decision | Scope |
| --- | --- |
| Keep | `pages.content` JSONB, `PUT /pages/{id}`, page CRUD/duplicate/ownership, record-preserving interactive mutations, content-free WebSocket invalidation/refetch, SEO/cache invalidation |
| Keep and simplify | `backend/internal/s_registry` section/component metadata, integration and interactive validators; the frontend lazy renderer registry, section defaults/add menu, unsupported fallback, `SectionFrame`, toolbar/action portals, literal appearance controls |
| Replace | Generated normalized `PageSection<T>` types and the bidirectional normalization adapter with a small document-envelope type plus safe config decode/update helpers that accept string or object input |
| Remove | Typed section/theme endpoints and DTOs, frontend `typedSectionApi` branch, normalized read/write service options and repository interfaces, shadow/cutover/projector code, normalized interactive repository/backfill, schema generator/contracts, page migration commands and feature flags |
| Remove after data proof | `page_themes`, `page_theme_tokens`, `page_section_instances`, `page_section_color_references`, `page_section_instance_items`, `page_section_presets`, `page_section_responses`, `page_section_response_migrations`, `page_section_quarantine`, and `page_section_shadow_runs` |
| Preserve | `page_sections`, `page_items`, `custom_sections`, datasource/component registry data, and JSON-backed interactive records |

`SectionFrame` and its controls are retained only as document-native UI. Palette
token support is migration-only in the reviewed tenant (zero tokens). Before it
is removed, every deployed tenant must be checked; any real token reference must
be resolved to its current literal color in the owning section config or block
that tenant's cleanup. No palette reference may silently become an inherited
color.

## Steps

1. **Freeze the actual document contract and run a lightweight production guard**
   - Record the operator-confirmed production fact that the normalized backfill,
     interactive-response migration, cutover audit, and root migration
     orchestrator were never run and that all pages still use `pages.content`.
   - Confirm the normalized flags remain off and run one metadata-only query per
     tenant for normalized response rows and palette tokens. Either being nonzero
     pauses only that tenant's cleanup; do not build a generalized migration
     program for empty shadow/telemetry state.
   - Take the normal pre-deployment database backup and a compact content
     manifest containing page ID, `content` hash, section count, and update time.
   - Add anonymized fixtures for the reviewed string/object config, numeric/string
     ID, `features`, empty page, custom/datasource, and interactive shapes.
   - If a normalized flag is unexpectedly enabled, disable it and verify one
     authorized page read and interactive write against `pages.content` before
     cleanup. Treat normalized-only responses or palette tokens as the only data
     exceptions needing a focused recovery decision.

2. **Make the JSON envelope authoritative and compatible**
   - Define one honest document boundary in Go and TypeScript: ordered section
     arrays; numeric-or-string section/item IDs; config as encoded string or JSON
     object on input; extra fields preserved; interactive runtime fields allowed
     only through their authorized paths.
   - Keep new builder-created IDs positive numeric values, but allow existing
     nonempty string IDs to render and round-trip. Interactive response routes
     may continue to require numeric IDs until separately redesigned.
   - Canonicalize `features` to the registered `feature_grid` renderer for
     validation/rendering without rewriting untouched stored content. New
     sections use canonical names.
   - Replace the normalized adapter with small config helpers that decode either
     input form, update owned keys without deleting unknown keys, and serialize
     only the section being edited. Do not split shell/config/runtime into a
     second persistence shape.
   - Add no-loss read/edit/save/duplicate/copy fixtures before deleting any
     compatibility code. Existing pages must no longer be stranded by the strict
     positive-integer/canonical-type save validator.

3. **Collapse backend runtime to one page path**
   - Remove normalized options and state from `main.go` and the page service:
     `PAGE_TYPED_SECTION_MUTATIONS`, `PAGE_NORMALIZED_INTERACTIVE_RESPONSES`,
     `PAGE_NORMALIZED_SECTION_READS`, and cutover thresholds.
   - Remove typed section/theme routes, handlers, service methods, DTO fields,
     revision gates, repositories, and tests. `GET/POST/PUT/DELETE
     /pages/{id}/sections*` and `PUT /pages/{id}/theme` must disappear; ordinary
     page CRUD remains unchanged.
   - Stop `Create` and `UpdatePreservingInteractive` from calling shadow sync or
     updating shadow telemetry. Page writes must touch only the authoritative
     page document and the existing domain effects.
   - Route all interactive reads/mutations through the existing locked-document
     implementation in `interactive.go`; remove the normalized response branch,
     repository, and readiness logic while preserving service policy and
     metadata-only audit/realtime behavior.
   - Remove `cmd/page-section-migrate`, all three backend page-section/response
     migration commands, and their operational references.

4. **Retain a useful registry without pretending configs are uniform**
   - Keep `List`, `Get`, `IsSupported`, canonical aliases, labels/groups,
     component bind points, integration-reference checks, and interactive config
     validation in `backend/internal/s_registry`.
   - Decouple registry definitions from normalized JSON Schemas, generated Go
     DTOs, and `GET /config/section-contracts`. Remove `section-contract-gen`,
     generated artifacts, the JSON-schema-only dependency, frontend contract
     scripts/format exception, and pre-commit contract-generation gate.
   - Retain only defaults/capabilities consumed by the JSON builder. Section-
     specific validation remains deliberately local: interactive and integration
     sections receive strong server validation; heterogeneous visual configs
     round-trip safely instead of being forced into a false universal schema.
   - Keep a lightweight drift test ensuring backend registered types, frontend
     add-menu metadata, and renderer keys agree, including the legacy alias.
     Avoid rebuilding a code-generation subsystem to test a 19-key registry.
   - Simplify the frontend renderer definition to the actual legacy `PageSection`
     contract while retaining lazy loading, exhaustive canonical renderer keys,
     per-type new-section defaults, and clipboard sanitizers.

5. **Preserve useful shared UI directly on page config**
   - Keep `SectionFrame`, single-toolbar ownership, action portals, layout,
     spacing, animation, collapse, content scaling, literal colors, and their
     focused accessibility/placement tests where they remain useful.
   - Rename migration-oriented helpers/comments and make shared controls read and
     patch the actual section config without constructing a normalized section.
   - Remove page-theme DTOs/API/tables and palette-mode UI after the tenant token
     preflight. Keep literal/inherit color behavior and unknown config keys.
   - Verify every block family, especially interactive, datasource, custom,
     rich-text, hero, gallery, and CTA, retains its real DOM behavior and config
     fields after the adapter/generator is removed.

6. **Retire normalized database state safely**
   - Add the next forward migration to drop only the typed-redesign tables after
     the lightweight guard and normal backup. Remove them from `001_schema.sql`, the
     soft-delete guard/trash inventories, test schema assertions, and fresh
     provisioning.
   - Remove migrations `030`-`033` from the replayed migration set once the
     forward cleanup migration covers already-deployed databases. Make migration
     `034` tolerate the normalized tables being absent so fresh installs do not
     recreate or reference them.
   - Do not drop `page_sections`, `page_items`, `custom_sections`, or unrelated
     orphan tables in this change.
   - Apply the cleanup first to an isolated database. Verify the content manifest,
     authorized page projections, interactive behavior, and schema before the
     normal tenant rollout. Do not invent a page-content migration: none is
     required for the authoritative JSON documents.

7. **Reconcile tests, specs, todos, and operational policy**
   - Rewrite the custom-page compatibility, interactive, multiplayer, backend,
     frontend, migration, route, realtime, caching, and security specs around one
     JSON authority. Remove claims that normalized cutover is complete or pending.
   - Reconcile `ui_component_refactor_plan` and
     `soft_delete_and_destructive_prompts` so future work does not rebuild or
     deploy the retired tables/contracts.
   - Preserve the useful historical findings from
     `typed_page_sections_redesign`, then retire that completed plan pair after
     this consolidation is verified.
   - Update `.routines/worker`, `.routines/correctness`, and `.routines/planner`
     remarks and commands that still treat normalized page sections as current.

## Verification

- Before/after content manifests match; the cleanup does not rewrite page
  documents or require content backfill.
- Existing object/string configs, numeric/string IDs, `features`, empty pages,
  custom/datasource sections, and all five interactive types render and survive
  an edit/save/reload cycle.
- Interactive guest/participant/editor/owner/admin tests preserve response
  limits, validation, idempotency, record privacy, moderation, concurrent
  builder-save/submit serialization, clipboard/duplicate clearing, and
  content-free realtime invalidation.
- Backend: `env GOCACHE=/tmp/skaia-go-cache GOTMPDIR=/tmp
  JWT_SECRET=test-secret go test ./...`; run focused race tests for page and
  interactive concurrent writes.
- Frontend: focused page/renderer/frame/interactive tests, full tests, lint,
  TypeScript, and production build.
- Run the complete migration chain twice on a clean database and once on an
  isolated clone containing normalized shadow rows. Confirm the final schema has
  the document tables but none of the retired typed-redesign tables.
- `rg` confirms removed flags, endpoints, commands, typed adapters, generated
  contracts, schemas, normalized tables, and stale spec/todo claims are absent
  except for the forward cleanup migration and concise historical trace.
- `git diff --check`, todo pairing/status checks, and linked-file checks pass.

